; A call to a polymorphic function answers the type it was APPLIED at.
;
; It did not. `checkApp` returned the arrow's result position
; unsubstituted, so `(-> a a)` instantiated to `(-> _t5 _t5)` and the
; call answered `_t5` - a minted variable `tyCompat` matches against
; everything. The consequence is that a polymorphic call laundered any
; value into any type:
;
;   (:: idf (-> a a))  (fn (idf x) x)
;   (strLen (idf 7))     ->  check OK, exit 139
;   (strLen 7)           ->  AX3004, correctly
;
; The same expression, one call apart, with opposite answers. And
; everything downstream inherited it: `match` arms whose type arrived
; through `vecGet` were never joined against each other, the
; exhaustiveness backstop's `Bool` exemption was reachable by passing
; the scrutinee through `idf`, and a lambda's parameter was never
; checked against what the lambda was applied to.
;
; The fix needs no substitution table. `tyInstVar` mints ONE node per
; (call site, source variable) and hands the same node back for every
; occurrence, so `(-> a a)` is `(-> _t5 _t5)` where both positions are
; the SAME node: overwriting it with the argument's type resolves the
; result too. Union-find where the union is the only operation and find
; is free. It runs in both directions - a parameter variable takes the
; argument's type, and an argument that is itself an unresolved
; variable takes the parameter's, which is what carries a type out of a
; lambda body - and it descends structurally, so a variable nested
; inside `(Option a)` or `(-> a Int)` is resolved as well.
;
; A binding always makes a type strictly more concrete: binding to
; another variable is refused, which is what keeps this cycle-free with
; no occurs check.
;
; Cost, measured over the 264-file clean corpus: two declarations that
; were imprecise rather than wrong. `BEnt.name` and `bsWith`'s second
; parameter each said `Int` while holding a binder's spelling that
; `bsFindFrom` compares with `strEq`; nothing could contradict them
; while a polymorphic result was an unconstrained variable. Both are
; corrected, and the sweep is clean.

(import Str)

(:: idf (-> a a))
(fn (idf x) x)

; Refused: `idf` was applied at `Int`, so it answers `Int`.
(:: viaCall (-> Int Int))
(fn (viaCall n) (strLen (idf n)))

; Refused for the same reason, one level in: the lambda's parameter is
; resolved by its own body, and the application then disagrees.
(:: ap (-> (-> a Int) a Int))
(fn (ap g x) (g x))

(:: viaLambda (-> Int Int))
(fn (viaLambda n) (ap (lambda (s) (strLen s)) n))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) 0)
